WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:06.470
Hey, I'm Zach.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:07.303
I'm Keith.

00:00:07.303 --> 00:00:08.150
I'm Ned.

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:09.653
And I'm Eugene.

00:00:09.653 --> 00:00:10.895
Zach: And we are The Try Guys.

00:00:10.895 --> 00:00:11.845
Eugene: And we are The Try Guys.

00:00:11.845 --> 00:00:15.590
Ned: And we're gonna tell you
how to become a YouTube star.

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:16.781
Here we go!

00:00:16.781 --> 00:00:18.480
Woo!

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:19.920
[Narrator] Here's how
to be a YouTube star,

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.580
featuring The Try Guys.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:22.750
Keith: Could you turn Ned down?

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:23.583
Zach: Yeah.
Keith: Is that something

00:00:23.583 --> 00:00:24.900
you can do?

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.390
[Narrator] The Try Guys
are huge YouTube stars.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:28.950
They have a massive fan base,

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:31.578
and millions of people watch
the four personalities,

00:00:31.578 --> 00:00:33.590
well, try anything.

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:36.920
First, they'll tell us why
their online formula works.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.653
Keith: Why do people like
to watch other people try?

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:44.280
Ned: I think you get to feel what we feel

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:47.370
even though you are not
trying, you're watching.

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:48.670
Zach: The core idea of trying things,

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:50.220
which is where we started from,

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.200
is just this idea of exploring
things that are familiar

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.172
to other people's passions and identities.

00:00:55.172 --> 00:00:57.590
So I think on that base
level there's an excitement,

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:59.970
if we are trying something
that you are familiar with,

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:02.110
we're either going to learn
something that you care about

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:03.935
or experience a struggle that you know

00:01:03.935 --> 00:01:05.484
that you haven't been able to communicate

00:01:05.484 --> 00:01:06.659
to the world at large.

00:01:06.659 --> 00:01:08.870
But then I think there's
also just a general interest

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:11.450
and excitement of four
guys who are willing

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:14.250
to go into things with an open mind

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.390
and see what the world has to offer.

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:18.750
[Narrator] So what's a
YouTube personality anyways?

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:20.350
Ned: I literally have no idea.

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:23.942
Keith: There are many types
of YouTube personalities.

00:01:23.942 --> 00:01:26.687
But probably the best are the ones that

00:01:26.687 --> 00:01:29.090
talk straight to camera,
talk about their life,

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:32.609
have a huge social meltdown,
and then apologize for it.

00:01:32.609 --> 00:01:33.442
Ned: Yeah.

00:01:33.442 --> 00:01:34.420
Keith: That's a really big thing.

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:35.287
Eugene: That's correct.

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:41.213
Ned: This may be the hardest
video I ever have to make.

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:43.040
I'm just...

00:01:46.928 --> 00:01:47.761
I'm so sorry.

00:01:49.477 --> 00:01:53.360
Keith: We found out that
our merch that we released

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.668
causes people to projectile vomit.

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:59.707
Ned: Excuse me while I sip some...

00:01:59.707 --> 00:02:01.390
Zach: Oh he's sipping some...
Keith: He's sipping tea.

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:03.390
Ned: Sipping tea! There it is.

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:06.010
Zach: OK, there are a couple
YouTube archetypes, right?

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:07.692
You've got the cutie,

00:02:07.692 --> 00:02:09.880
who's just, like, innocent, bubbly, fun.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:11.400
"Hey guys! What's going on?"

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:12.520
You've got the prankster,

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.240
you know, the old jokester.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.970
The guy that just likes
to mess with his friends.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:20.850
There are the gurus, people
that you turn to for advice,

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:23.800
the ones who feel like they
got it figured all out,

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:24.633
Keith.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:28.215
Keith: Then you have hot twins.

00:02:28.215 --> 00:02:29.181
Zach: Hot twins.
Keith: We only have a

00:02:29.181 --> 00:02:30.207
half of our...

00:02:30.207 --> 00:02:31.040
Your twin couldn't make it.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:32.148
Ned: Oh, what if you had a twin?

00:02:32.148 --> 00:02:34.431
You'd be so much more famous.

00:02:34.431 --> 00:02:36.540
Well, you'd have to divide by two though.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:38.290
Zach: Probably our favorite
subgenre of YouTube

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:39.530
is the hot-twin subgenre.

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:43.100
There are so many, just,
attractive twin couplets.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:44.700
And I don't understand
where they all come from

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:45.840
and how they all figure out

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.540
that, just, YouTube is where they belong.

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.570
Ned: We were in a museum, actually,

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:53.280
and the category of the exhibit
was audience surrogates.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.260
Zach: Oh, like, literally
us were in the museum.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.200
Ned: Yeah, The Try Guys were in a museum,

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.590
not we were walking in
a museum the other day.

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:01.110
Keith: That's what I
thought you were saying.

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:02.492
Eugene: You know, I like to go to museums

00:03:02.492 --> 00:03:03.824
by myself sometimes.
Zach: Yeah!

00:03:03.824 --> 00:03:04.782
Eugene: Do you guys do that?
Zach: Yeah!

00:03:04.782 --> 00:03:05.615
Ned: Yeah.
Keith: No.

00:03:05.615 --> 00:03:06.448
Ned: Well, I used to.
Eugene: But a few children

00:03:06.448 --> 00:03:08.463
stopped me at the Natural History Museum.

00:03:08.463 --> 00:03:09.338
Zach: Ah, so good, a lot of

00:03:09.338 --> 00:03:10.196
their bones.
Eugene: I was looking at

00:03:10.196 --> 00:03:12.355
the bones, and they said, "What
are you doing by yourself?"

00:03:12.355 --> 00:03:14.127
Zach: One of the best collections
of bones in the world.

00:03:14.127 --> 00:03:15.026
Eugene: I didn't know what to say,

00:03:15.026 --> 00:03:17.355
and I said, "It's OK
to be alone sometimes."

00:03:17.355 --> 00:03:19.120
And that sounded so cryptic.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:21.845
And...but you know what? It's
OK to be alone sometimes.

00:03:21.845 --> 00:03:24.062
Ned: See? That's how you
become a YouTube star:

00:03:24.062 --> 00:03:25.103
authenticity.

00:03:25.103 --> 00:03:29.232
Which is why I wanna tell you
about The Try Guys' new merch.

00:03:29.232 --> 00:03:30.615
Zach: That is, to be a YouTuber,

00:03:30.615 --> 00:03:33.293
you have to be able to
plug at the drop of a hat.

00:03:34.957 --> 00:03:36.023
[Narrator] Vulnerability.

00:03:36.023 --> 00:03:37.982
What is it, and why is it important?

00:03:37.982 --> 00:03:40.100
Keith: The reason we are
vulnerable in our videos

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:43.340
is because it is OK to
be vulnerable in life.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:45.258
And I think we wanna demonstrate that

00:03:45.258 --> 00:03:47.010
that kind of thing is good. It's healthy.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:49.180
And it allows you to be
closer to one another

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:50.960
when you're vulnerable around one another.

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:53.240
It just helps destigmatize this idea

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:54.930
of showing your emotions.

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:58.413
Ned: I think it stands in stark
contrast to what is, like,

00:03:58.413 --> 00:04:00.690
a typical view of masculinity

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:03.600
where you're supposed to be
stoic and not show any emotions.

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:08.100
Oftentimes tackle, like, very
difficult personal subjects

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:12.217
and process it by talking
about it on YouTube.

00:04:12.217 --> 00:04:13.050
Woo!

00:04:14.233 --> 00:04:15.749
Zach: Being vulnerable I think allows you

00:04:15.749 --> 00:04:17.599
to take the mask off

00:04:17.599 --> 00:04:21.400
and present what is hopefully
a truer version of yourself.

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:22.483
It allows you to be raw.

00:04:22.483 --> 00:04:24.658
It allows you to be in that place,

00:04:24.658 --> 00:04:25.750
and that place of vulnerability

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:27.470
is where you can make mistakes,

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:29.540
where you can admit to failures,

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:30.830
and that's where growth can occur.

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:31.663
Eugene: It's kind of hard, because

00:04:31.663 --> 00:04:32.638
everything is voyeuristic now,

00:04:32.638 --> 00:04:34.200
and everything is, like, in your face.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.775
Social media has sort of
distorted what we think is real.

00:04:36.775 --> 00:04:38.992
So sometimes it's really
hard to even interpret

00:04:38.992 --> 00:04:41.370
or understand if someone is
being completely themselves.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:44.002
If you are giving 100% of
yourself in that moment,

00:04:44.002 --> 00:04:46.068
then people can smell it.

00:04:46.068 --> 00:04:48.098
Especially the younger
generation. They know.

00:04:48.098 --> 00:04:49.990
'Cause I think we're
constantly bombarded now

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:51.625
by ideas of authenticity

00:04:51.625 --> 00:04:54.900
when really it's just
sometimes to sell something.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:56.400
It's not just branding of authenticity,

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:58.303
it's actually being authentic,

00:04:58.303 --> 00:05:00.416
and I think that's good
for young kids to remember

00:05:00.416 --> 00:05:02.890
when they're starting to film themselves,

00:05:02.890 --> 00:05:04.695
just because that's a
difficult thing to do

00:05:04.695 --> 00:05:06.050
when you're really young.

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:07.510
[Narrator] What about oversharing?

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:09.870
How much of your life
should you keep to yourself?

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:11.260
Zach: I think the four
of us have struggled with

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:14.110
what amount of our full selves
we should put out online.

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:16.212
Like, I kept that fact
that I had a girlfriend

00:05:16.212 --> 00:05:17.900
secret from the internet for two years,

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:20.450
just because I wanted to
have that corner of my life

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:23.330
where I was, you know, growing
and experiencing something

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:27.095
organically, without it being
seen under the microscope

00:05:27.095 --> 00:05:28.435
of what, like, people thought about her

00:05:28.435 --> 00:05:30.530
and having to subject her to that.

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:32.550
On one hand, we have
this amazing opportunity

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:34.530
to share our lives and to affect people

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:36.485
and to show our vulnerabilities

00:05:36.485 --> 00:05:37.401
and things that we struggle with

00:05:37.401 --> 00:05:38.742
that maybe you're struggling with too.

00:05:38.742 --> 00:05:41.640
But also, you wanna
have a part of your life

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:42.940
that is private and separate,

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:46.708
especially when it comes to
relationships and family.

00:05:46.708 --> 00:05:48.342
Keith: But it's tougher when, you know,

00:05:48.342 --> 00:05:50.250
you're famous for being you.

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:52.810
I mean, for the most part,
like, if you meet me in public,

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:54.858
I'm pretty much the same
person you see on a video.

00:05:54.858 --> 00:05:55.949
Like, I'm me,

00:05:55.949 --> 00:05:57.141
for the most part.
Zach: I'm a little dumber

00:05:57.141 --> 00:05:58.335
on camera, but not much.
Keith: Yeah he's a little

00:05:58.335 --> 00:05:59.985
dumber on camera.
Ned: I'm a lot dumber

00:05:59.985 --> 00:06:01.580
on camera.
Keith: That's for sure.

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:03.370
But you'll have to find out for yourself.

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:05.642
Zach: When you become digitally famous.

00:06:05.642 --> 00:06:08.784
Eugene: Other people should
not own your contents.

00:06:08.784 --> 00:06:09.858
They can own your content,

00:06:09.858 --> 00:06:12.185
but they can't own the
content of your character.

00:06:12.185 --> 00:06:14.152
Zach: Everyone get a box.

00:06:14.152 --> 00:06:15.467
Eugene: An actual physical box.

00:06:15.467 --> 00:06:17.900
Ned: Write down all your secrets.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:19.246
Take out two.

00:06:19.246 --> 00:06:20.910
Zach: Then bury the box.

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:23.040
Make a treasure map.

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:25.550
Give it to future generations.

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:26.900
They're gonna have the time of their life.

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:30.190
Oh my God, if you discovered
a treasure map right now,

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:31.900
we would all drop
everything and go, right?

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:33.360
Keith: Right? I wish.

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:34.251
Zach: Oh my God.

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:37.060
[Narrator] Now it's time
to put it all together.

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:39.858
We had Ned, Eugene, Keith, and Zach

00:06:39.858 --> 00:06:42.151
demonstrate the Try Guys method of success

00:06:42.151 --> 00:06:43.747
with two inventions:

00:06:43.747 --> 00:06:45.846
the spider catcher, and the fruit peeler.

00:06:45.846 --> 00:06:47.773
Ned: It's pink. It's got brushes.

00:06:47.773 --> 00:06:50.739
Zach: This is called the Critter Catcher.

00:06:50.739 --> 00:06:51.949
Ned: My Critter Catcher.

00:06:51.949 --> 00:06:53.099
Zach: Nice. Nailed it.

00:06:53.099 --> 00:06:54.140
Eugene: And this is the critter.

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:55.315
Keith: That's the critter.

00:06:55.315 --> 00:06:56.920
Zach: So the idea...can
I see what the gun does?

00:06:56.920 --> 00:06:58.344
Ned: Can I be the critter?

00:06:58.344 --> 00:06:59.850
Zach: Wait, open it up. Oh!

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:01.806
Ned: Ow! I've been caught!
Keith: Oh wow.

00:07:01.806 --> 00:07:03.060
Zach: I like...can you
do that right to camera?

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:05.110
It looks like an alien's face.

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:06.748
Keith: This is awesome. Look at this.

00:07:06.748 --> 00:07:10.058
It seems like an old cartoon
that would just say "BAM!"

00:07:10.058 --> 00:07:11.408
Bam!
Zach: I don't even want it

00:07:11.408 --> 00:07:13.207
for bugs. I just wanna play with it.

00:07:13.207 --> 00:07:14.910
Keith: I'm not a murderer!

00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:17.740
I'll use my plastic tentacles!

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:18.933
Zach: Hey, it works!

00:07:18.933 --> 00:07:20.074
That's cool.
Keith: Wow, look at him.

00:07:20.074 --> 00:07:21.307
He's in there.
Ned: Pretty almost.

00:07:21.307 --> 00:07:22.598
Keith: But if he was wriggling, I think

00:07:22.598 --> 00:07:23.826
he could get out of this.
Ned: Yeah.

00:07:23.826 --> 00:07:25.675
Eugene: Wait, do you
guys actually kill bugs

00:07:25.675 --> 00:07:27.196
when you see one? I can't kill.

00:07:27.196 --> 00:07:28.813
Zach: What kind of bugs?
Eugene: Any bugs.

00:07:28.813 --> 00:07:29.933
Zach: Well you can't kill cockroaches

00:07:29.933 --> 00:07:33.300
because then other cockroaches
smell it, and they come.

00:07:36.552 --> 00:07:39.538
Ned: Flying cockroach in the wild.

00:07:39.538 --> 00:07:41.443
Zach: Grr, I'm mad!

00:07:42.972 --> 00:07:44.224
Keith: I have a lot of forward thrust.

00:07:44.224 --> 00:07:46.432
So yeah, that time I
didn't thrust hard enough.

00:07:46.432 --> 00:07:48.390
All: Ayy!

00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:49.560
Zach: Oh no!

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:51.082
Ned: All right, what's next?

00:07:51.082 --> 00:07:53.620
Zach: This is the Orange
Peel Professional.

00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:56.226
Not to be confused with
the Orange Peel Amateur.

00:07:56.226 --> 00:07:57.142
Ned: Oh boy.

00:07:57.142 --> 00:07:58.033
Zach: Wow.

00:07:58.033 --> 00:08:00.030
This is gonna get juice everywhere, right?

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:01.147
Ned: Put it on the carousel.

00:08:01.147 --> 00:08:02.130
Eugene: Yeah, stab that down.

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:03.129
Ned: Poke it in the top.

00:08:03.129 --> 00:08:05.486
Eugene: Put it right on the
top, so lean it in, lean it in

00:08:05.486 --> 00:08:07.217
a bit. There we go. Yes!
Zach: Oh, through that.

00:08:07.217 --> 00:08:08.092
Eugene: Always do it in

00:08:08.092 --> 00:08:09.241
the hole.
Keith: Oh, don't go

00:08:09.241 --> 00:08:10.570
through the, the thing!

00:08:10.570 --> 00:08:12.179
Ned: Sometimes you gotta push hard.

00:08:13.279 --> 00:08:15.082
Zach: There are two buttons.
There's a one and a two.

00:08:15.082 --> 00:08:16.197
Ned: Let's go two.

00:08:17.349 --> 00:08:18.982
Keith: Let's ramp it up to two.

00:08:18.982 --> 00:08:21.410
Zach: Oh God!
All: Whoa!

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:25.048
Zach: Oh, but it didn't...

00:08:25.048 --> 00:08:26.483
[Producer] You had it started from the...

00:08:26.483 --> 00:08:27.609
Zach: Oh, Ned!

00:08:27.609 --> 00:08:28.587
Keith: Oh!

00:08:28.587 --> 00:08:29.997
Ned: I think that's my fault.
Keith: If it was really a

00:08:29.997 --> 00:08:32.495
professional, it would've
known where it was starting.

00:08:32.495 --> 00:08:34.195
Zach: Maybe one will go backwards?

00:08:37.653 --> 00:08:38.976
That is very satisfying.

00:08:40.614 --> 00:08:42.045
Keith: When you keep
going will it take off...

00:08:42.045 --> 00:08:43.494
Oh! It's taking off the white part now!

00:08:43.494 --> 00:08:44.577
Oh!

00:08:46.106 --> 00:08:46.939
Eugene: Oh!
Ned: Oh, it's just going

00:08:46.939 --> 00:08:48.222
back and forth.
Keith: Let's keep running it!

00:08:48.222 --> 00:08:49.704
Yeah baby!

00:08:50.962 --> 00:08:53.778
Ned: Keep this orange string going!

00:08:57.003 --> 00:08:57.894
Yeah!

00:08:59.693 --> 00:09:02.526
Keith: Oh, she looks good!

00:09:02.526 --> 00:09:04.559
Yes baby!
Ned: Orange juice!

00:09:04.559 --> 00:09:06.280
Keith: Oh! You look good, girl!

00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:08.617
Ned: Oh, you're looking like a snack!

00:09:08.617 --> 00:09:10.050
Keith: Yes!

00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:11.766
You are skinny!
Zach: Uh oh, uh oh,

00:09:11.766 --> 00:09:13.141
I'm gonna need a paper
towel, I'm gonna need

00:09:13.141 --> 00:09:15.435
a paper towel right now.
Ned: Slurp it! Slurp it, Zach!

00:09:15.435 --> 00:09:16.268
